<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Kos</h1><p class="st-meta">900 BCE – 129 BCE (approx.) · capital Kos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Dorian island state off Anatolia, birthplace of Hippocrates and home of the famous healing sanctuary of Asclepius.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kos, an island off the Carian coast near <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/halicarnassus">Halicarnassus</a>, was settled from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, with Mycenaean remains. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorians">Dorian Greeks</a> occupied it in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, and it joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorian-hexapolis">Dorian Hexapolis</a> with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/knidos">Knidos</a>, Halicarnassus and the Rhodian cities. Its first chief town was Astypalaia in the south-west.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kos was known for wine and fine translucent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> cloth. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippocrates">Hippocrates</a> was born there c. 460 BCE and the Coan school of medicine bears his name. After Persian rule the island joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delian-league">Delian League</a>. In 366 BCE the Coans founded a new capital on the north-east tip, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asclepieion-of-kos">Asclepieion</a> was later built nearby.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Kos was a favoured base of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-ii-philadelphus">Ptolemy II</a> was born and the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philitas-of-cos">Philitas</a> taught. It later allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and became part of the province of Asia in 129 BCE, remaining a free city prized for its sanctuary.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 900 BCE – 129 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60